WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 8.4 · Marketing & Communications
Storytelling and ethical storytelling

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Tell the story of the problem and why you started, putting people's dignity and safety first.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:26.652
Stories help people understand change in a way numbers alone cannot. Ethical storytelling puts the dignity, safety and agency of the people in the story first, before the needs of your launch.

4
00:00:27.052 --> 00:00:27.652
Three rules for ethical stories

5
00:00:27.652 --> 00:00:39.722
Informed consent: People know where it will be used, for how long, and that they can withdraw. For example: A trainee baker agrees to a photo on the website for one year.

6
00:00:39.722 --> 00:00:49.941
Their own words: People tell their story themselves where possible and see the final version. For example: A trainee reads and approves her quote before it is posted.

7
00:00:49.941 --> 00:01:02.841
Protect from harm: Change or remove identifying details, and do not publish when the risk outweighs the benefit. For example: A trainee’s street and old school are left out of the story.

8
00:01:03.241 --> 00:01:03.841
Stories you can tell before launch

9
00:01:03.841 --> 00:01:11.367
The problem, told truthfully: young people here who left school early cannot find a first job.

10
00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:18.152
Why we started: a baker and a youth worker who saw the same gap.

11
00:01:18.152 --> 00:01:25.567
The first neighbours who tasted our bread, and the first café that ordered.

12
00:01:25.967 --> 00:01:26.567
Why it matters

13
00:01:26.567 --> 00:01:31.500
1. Stories make change real for funders, supporters and customers.

14
00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:37.174
2. Rules set before your first post are easier than fixing habits.

15
00:01:37.174 --> 00:01:43.107
3. Breaking someone’s trust can harm them and end yours.

16
00:01:43.507 --> 00:01:44.107
Where it applies

17
00:01:44.107 --> 00:01:48.300
1. Your website, newsletters, campaigns and press work.

18
00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:52.863
2. Your consent log, checked before anything is published.

19
00:01:52.863 --> 00:01:59.167
3. Your proof points, alongside the numbers from your MEAL data.

20
00:01:59.567 --> 00:02:00.167
What to avoid

21
00:02:00.167 --> 00:02:04.730
Images and words that show people as helpless.

22
00:02:04.730 --> 00:02:09.293
Publishing details that could identify someone at risk.

23
00:02:09.293 --> 00:02:15.967
Losing track of consent: keep a log from the first story.

24
00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:18.367
You write your storytelling rules and set up a consent log.

25
00:02:18.367 --> 00:02:22.367
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

26
00:02:23.867 --> 00:02:26.967
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
